A real-mode interrupt changes SP only, as on x86: call_isr set ESP to (ESP - 6) & 0xffff with a 16-bit stack,
clearing ESP's upper half (code that keeps a 32-bit value in ESP across a real-mode interrupt, as a DOS extender's
real-mode calls do, saw it cleared). The firmware's fast path did it right. Sent upstream as hchunhui/tiny386#59.

--- a/i386.c
+++ b/i386.c
@@ -4704,7 +4704,7 @@
 		saddr16(&meml1, cpu->flags);
 		saddr16(&meml2, cpu->seg[SEG_CS].sel);
 		saddr16(&meml3, cpu->ip);
-		sreg32(4, (sp - 2 * 3) & sp_mask);
+		set_sp(sp - 2 * 3, sp_mask);
 
 		TRY1(set_seg(cpu, SEG_CS, newcs));
 		cpu->next_ip = newip;
